  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCold front will continue its slow progression across Michigan, \nbringing additional rounds of showers and perhaps some embedded \nthunderstorms. Guidance continues to suggest the front will \ntrack southeastward, but perhaps it may stall near Saginaw Bay? \nHowever, precipitation should diminish/ move largely out of the \narea by at least early afternoon if not sooner (some lingering \nchances near Saginaw Bay if the front does stall in that \nvicinity as being advertised). Northeast Lower, remains the area\nmost favored for more locally heavy rainfall today, where more \nrounds of showers look to track across the region into perhaps \nthe early afternoon, especailly far southeast areas near Saginaw\nBay. Locations across northeast Lower have reported receiving \naround roughly 1 to 2 inches from last evening/ last nights \nshowers. A few showers look possible Friday afternoon/evening \nmainly across northeast Lower between the departing front and \nthe next incoming disturbance.\n\nForecast thinking beyond Friday remains largely unchanged. A \nbroader longwave trough is still expected to become established \nacross the Great Lakes through the weekend and into early next \nweek, keeping any meaningful heat displaced to our south while \nmaintaining a generally west to northwest flow aloft. Within \nthat flow, subtle shortwaves are expected to rotate through the \nregion, although confidence remains fairly low regarding the \nexact timing and placement of these disturbances. As a result, \nmaintaining periodic chances for showers and a few thunderstorms\nfrom Friday night through Monday. Temperatures are expected to \nremain seasonably cool for early August, though highs could \ntrend a bit lower should a more persistent cloud cover and \nshowery scenario ultimately materializes.",
